From: well0nez <8986239+well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: media: cedrus: a job of another codec starts from a reset engine

With VP9 on the VE (0292), an H.265 stream decoded next to a VP9 one
fails every job: "Failed to setup decoding job: -22", and GStreamer hands
out 300 identical, undecoded pictures with exit 0. Alone, the same H.265
clip is bit-exact; after the VP9 stream ends it is bit-exact again once
runtime PM has reset the engine between streams.

VP9 runs on the H.265 engine and programs registers there that H.265
never writes. One is +0x504, where VP9 puts its frame header with bits 31
and 26 always set; H.265 writes that register only after it has read the
slice header through the engine's bit reader, so the reader runs in
whatever mode VP9 left and returns the zero byte cedrus_h265_setup()
rejects. Clearing +0x504 before the read ended the -EINVAL but not the
wrong pictures (0/300), and clearing the other registers only VP9 writes
(+0x51c, +0x590..+0x5ac) did not either: the rest of what VP9 leaves is
not found. A reset between two jobs of different codecs ends both, the
way the engine is reset between streams anyway.

Every codec programs the engine in full for each job (VP9's probability
adaptation is the CPU's), so the reset loses nothing. Measured on the
HY310, 13.10.2026, two streams at a time, both compared frame by frame
against their software references: VP8+H.265, VP9+H.265, MPEG-2+H.264,
VP8+VP9 and H.265 10-bit+VP9, two rounds, all bit-exact and no driver
error; the same run without this patch fails VP9+H.265 and H.265
10-bit+VP9 in both rounds (0/300, 300-304 setup errors each). Three streams
at once (H.264 1080p, H.265 1080p, VP9 720p) are bit-exact.

Not cstenger's: found while porting his series (13.10.2026); it belongs
in front of his VP9 work wherever that goes.
---
diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
@@ -268,6 +268,8 @@ struct cedrus_dev {
 
 	struct reset_control	*rstc;
 	struct reset_control	*rstc_ve3;
+	/* pixel format of the last job, to reset the engine on a codec switch */
+	u32			last_pixfmt;
 
 	unsigned int		capabilities;
 
diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_dec.c b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_dec.c
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_dec.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_dec.c
@@ -13,6 +13,7 @@
  * Marek Szyprowski, <m.szyprowski@samsung.com>
  */
 
+#include <linux/reset.h>
 #include <media/v4l2-device.h>
 #include <media/v4l2-ioctl.h>
 #include <media/v4l2-event.h>
@@ -99,6 +100,21 @@ void cedrus_device_run(void *priv)
 
 	v4l2_m2m_buf_copy_metadata(run.src, run.dst, true);
 
+	/*
+	 * A job of another codec than the last one starts from a reset
+	 * engine. VP9 runs on the H.265 engine and leaves registers set that
+	 * H.265 never writes - its frame header at +0x504 with bits 31 and 26
+	 * set, read by the bit reader before H.265 writes that register - so
+	 * an H.265 job after a VP9 one failed its slice header (-EINVAL)
+	 * until runtime PM reset the engine between streams. Clearing that
+	 * register alone ended the errors but not the wrong pictures; a reset
+	 * on the switch ends both. Every codec programs the engine in full for
+	 * each job (VP9's adaptation is the CPU's), so nothing is lost.
+	 */
+	if (dev->last_pixfmt && dev->last_pixfmt != ctx->src_fmt.pixelformat)
+		reset_control_reset(dev->rstc);
+	dev->last_pixfmt = ctx->src_fmt.pixelformat;
+
 	/*
 	 * The primary output describes the RECONSTRUCTION, which is the coded
 	 * size.  With the scaler off that is dst_fmt; with it on, dst_fmt is
